  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nCurrently, S/WV mid level trough moving east into the western \nDakotas this aft, with its associated sfc low/trough from south\ncentral South Dakota north into central ND. Best lift (and thus\nprecipitation coverage and intensity) with this wave is on the \nnorth side of the sfc trough axis over northern areas of the\nstate where low level frontogensis forcing combines with the \nupper level forcing of the wave. Sfc observations are reporting\nseveral hundreths of accumulation so far associated with the\nmore moderate rain. Rain will continue to track from west to \neast, ending this evening along with clouds diminishing west \ninto central.\n\nNorthern stream S/WV developing south-southeast across Manitoba\ninto Alberta today and tonight will bring a backdoor cold front\ninto the region from the northeast tonight into Wednesday, with\nboth the RRFS and RAP depicting low level clouds accompanying\nthis boundary into the Turtle Mountain region 6-12Z, then\nexpanding south and west Wednesday morning. Ahead of this \nboundary tonight, winds will be light and low level moisture \nelevated, and coupled with decreasing clouds fog development is\nfavored over northwest and central areas as per the RRFS and \nRAP. This is in the gridded forecast and also added it to the \nHWO. This will be monitored, with more enhanced messaging sent \nas needed later tonight once fog develops. \n\nWednesday and Thursday will see sharp upper level ridging from\nthe Southern Rockies north across the Northern High Plains to\nour west, and a deep upper level trough/closed low to our east.\nThis sets up northerly flow aloft across the Dakotas and will\nalso result in the aforementioned backdoor cold front stalling\nacross the central Dakotas. Mild temperatures will be ahead of\nthis boundary to the west, with colder temperatures to the east.\nAlong this baroclinic zone, we may also see further fog and/or\nstratus development again Wed night and even Thu night. Elevated\ncloud cover is also a possibility.\n\nThis pattern slowly breaks down late this week into next \nweekend, with temperatures remaining mild to the west and \nmoderating some central and east. Still looks mainly dry though\nmay see some precipitation activity returning for the later\nparts of this weekend. \n\nNext week is looking more active as a long wave upper level\ntrough moves into the western CONUS early on, developing \neastward with time.",
